{:edb/db-before-t 2148 :edb/db-after-t 2149 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535461 50 #inst "2026-10-09T00:35:17.180Z" 13194139535461 true] [17592186119586 1100 "Determine the dimension of\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/assets/c0a1d1860f5be98c6606f191fd26b0f799bc182b7b4e7fdca542d693290f11e4.png)" 13194139535461 true] [17592186119586 1100 "Determine the dimension of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535461 false] [17592186119586 1145 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form. We see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $2$nd columns. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* two vectors. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/assets/268ea5cffffb0765f5c4bbacff7f3253ad4243e0e1ffa77d3a8a5a7f794d62ba.png)" 13194139535461 true] [17592186119586 1145 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form. We see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $2$nd columns. This tells us that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}\n$$\n\nforms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* two vectors. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535461 false] [17592186119587 1100 "Determine the dimension of\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/assets/668c8e17567f5635f3d39c98dced5c77c60a287cac8a92d29db2e8afefe94cec.png)" 13194139535461 true] [17592186119587 1100 "Determine the dimension of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535461 false] [17592186119587 1145 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + 3R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has one pivot columns: the $1$st column. This tells us that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{v}_1 \\}\n$$\n\nforms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of one vector, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* one vector. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535461 false] [17592186119587 1145 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has one pivot columns: the $1$st column. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of one vector, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* one vector. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/assets/4196e42d01f879b24974be9c0d1bc456c4894cb19b832efab46f3cdb8a28b644.png)" 13194139535461 true] [17592186119769 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350412 13194139535461 true] [17592186119769 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350447 13194139535461 true] [17592186119769 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350454 13194139535461 true] [17592186119769 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350461 13194139535461 true] [17592186119769 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350468 13194139535461 true] [17592186119770 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350419 13194139535461 true] [17592186119770 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350426 13194139535461 true] [17592186119770 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350433 13194139535461 true] [17592186119770 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350440 13194139535461 true] [17592186120297 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535461 true] [17592186120297 1100 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=$" 13194139535461 true] [17592186120297 1100 "What is $dim(Span{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}})$?" 13194139535461 false] [17592186120297 1145 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=1.$" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350412 1017 #edb/ref 17592186350413 13194139535461 true] [17592186350412 1089 "q-159784" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350412 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535461 true] [17592186350412 1100 "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=4$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=3$" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350412 1113 #uuid "4bbe3484-6ec6-5a37-a5e3-f6675f0e9f72" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350412 1145 "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n\n- Statement II is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3$ (not $4$).\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=2$ (not $3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350414 13194139535461 true] [17592186350413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350415 13194139535461 true] [17592186350413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350416 13194139535461 true] [17592186350413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350417 13194139535461 true] [17592186350413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350418 13194139535461 true] [17592186350413 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535461 true] [17592186350413 1041 "selection" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350413 1104 #edb/ref 17592186350416 13194139535461 true] [17592186350413 1110 #uuid "b6d0145b-6384-5187-b500-9c183f001d2d" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350414 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350414 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535461 true] [17592186350414 1111 #uuid "7e3f340c-ed19-50cb-8196-45eb2e65caad" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350415 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350415 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535461 true] [17592186350415 1111 #uuid "2d770e5d-5fe9-58f8-83a8-345cd94e0423" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350416 1034 "I only" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350416 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535461 true] [17592186350416 1111 #uuid "dec7751d-4aa9-522e-ab3f-2242cdaf5500" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350417 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350417 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535461 true] [17592186350417 1111 #uuid "44b2ed1d-0846-5bf2-8713-bfb3b98c2be7" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350418 1034 "II only" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350418 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535461 true] [17592186350418 1111 #uuid "7e4c6d3c-277f-5519-b973-23ed4c58062b" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350419 1017 #edb/ref 17592186350420 13194139535461 true] [17592186350419 1089 "q-35292" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350419 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535461 true] [17592186350419 1100 "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}.$" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350419 1113 #uuid "2cc5280e-8de7-5945-a4e2-7c56b5047caa" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350419 1145 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -44 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{22}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -44 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350421 13194139535461 true] [17592186350420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350422 13194139535461 true] [17592186350420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350423 13194139535461 true] [17592186350420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350424 13194139535461 true] [17592186350420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350425 13194139535461 true] [17592186350420 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535461 true] [17592186350420 1041 "selection" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350420 1104 #edb/ref 17592186350423 13194139535461 true] [17592186350420 1110 #uuid "195293fd-16ff-549f-baec-d75faf4d0827" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350421 1034 "9" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350421 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535461 true] [17592186350421 1111 #uuid "9d1004ba-640e-53a1-9fb2-2981c375e44e" 13194139535461 true] [17592186350422 1034 "3" 131